+sivapithecus | 12.2 mya | Sivalik Hills | nature, science | himalaya | Sivapithecus | Apes of the Siwaliks | Sivapithecus is a genus of extinct apes. Fossil remains now assigned to it, the oldest dated to 12.2 million years ago in the Miocene, have been found since the 19th century in the Sivalik Hills of the Indian subcontinent and in Kutch. | Twelve million years ago apes called Sivapithecus lived in South Asia. Their fossils have been found in the Sivalik Hills.
+toba-jwalapuram | 74000 BCE | Jwalapuram | nature, technology | south | Jwalapuram | Before and after the Toba eruption | At Jwalapuram in Andhra Pradesh, stone tools lie both under and over layers of volcanic ash left by the Toba eruption of about 74,000 BC, showing habitation before and after the event. | About 76,000 years ago a giant volcano called Toba erupted, and its ash fell on India. At Jwalapuram, stone tools lie both below the ash and above it.
+narmada-human | 1982 | Narmada River | science, nature | central | Narmada Human | The Narmada skull is found | The Narmada Human is an extinct human that lived in central India during the Middle and Late Pleistocene. It is known from a skull cap discovered on the bank of the Narmada River in Madhya Pradesh in 1982 by Arun Sonakia. | In 1982 a scientist found part of a very old human skull beside the Narmada River. It belonged to an ancient kind of human.
+bhimbetka | 10000 BCE | Bhimbetka rock shelters | arts, daily | central | Bhimbetka rock shelters | The Bhimbetka paintings | The Bhimbetka rock shelters in central India span the Paleolithic and Mesolithic periods and show the earliest traces of human life in India. The earliest of their cave paintings are dated to 10,000 BCE. | In rock shelters at Bhimbetka, people painted pictures on the walls about 12,000 years ago.
+lahuradewa | 9000 BCE | Lahuradewa | food, technology | gangetic | Lahuradewa | Early pottery at Lahuradewa | Lahuradewa, in the Upper Gangetic Plain of Uttar Pradesh, is noted to have been occupied as early as 9,000 BCE. It provides the oldest evidence of ceramics in the Indian subcontinent. | At Lahuradewa in the Ganges plain, archaeologists found the oldest pottery known in the Indian subcontinent.
+edakkal | 6000 BCE | Edakkal caves | arts | south | Edakkal caves | The Edakkal carvings | Inside the Edakkal caves, in the Wayanad district of Kerala, are petroglyphs believed to date to at least 6,000 BCE, from the Neolithic period. | In caves high in the hills of Kerala, people carved pictures into the rock at least 8,000 years ago.
+amri | 3600 BCE | Amri, Sindh | daily, technology | pakistan | Amri, Sindh | The settlement of Amri | Amri is an ancient settlement in today's Sindh, south of Mohenjo-daro, that goes back to 3600 BCE. | Amri in Sindh is an old village site that goes back about 5,600 years.
+kot-diji | 3300 BCE | Kot Diji | daily, power | pakistan | Kot Diji | Kot Diji | Kot Diji, about 45 km south of Khairpur in Sindh, is an ancient site of the Indus Valley Civilisation, estimated to have been occupied around 3300 BCE. | Kot Diji was a town of the early Indus people, lived in about 5,300 years ago.
+burzahom | 3000 BCE..1000 BCE | Burzahom archaeological site | food, daily | himalaya | Burzahom archaeological site | The farmers of Burzahom | The Burzahom site in the Kashmir Valley has revealed four phases of culture between 3000 BCE and 1000 BCE. Evidence of wheat was found there. | At Burzahom in Kashmir, early farmers lived from about 5,000 years ago. They grew wheat.
+ahar-banas | 3000 BCE..1500 BCE | Balathal | technology, daily | north | Ahar–Banas culture; Balathal | The Ahar–Banas culture | The Ahar or Banas culture was a Chalcolithic culture of south-eastern Rajasthan, lasting from about 3000 to 1500 BCE, contemporary with and adjacent to the Indus Valley civilisation. Balathal is one of its ninety known sites. | While the Indus cities flourished, farming villages that used copper grew up next door in Rajasthan.
+chirand | 2500 BCE | Chirand | food, daily | gangetic | Chirand | The village mound of Chirand | Chirand, on the northern bank of the Ganga in Bihar, has a large prehistoric mound with Neolithic, Chalcolithic and Iron Age settlements stacked in layers, dating from 2500 BC. Evidence of wheat was found. | At Chirand beside the Ganga, people lived in the same spot for thousands of years. Each village was built on top of the last.
+harappa | 2600 BCE | Harappa | daily, trade, power | pakistan | Harappa; Indus Valley Civilisation | Harappa | Harappa was a settlement in the Punjab, near a former course of the Ravi River. It gave its name to the Harappan, or Indus Valley, civilisation, whose mature phase lasted from 2600 to 1900 BCE. | Harappa was one of the great cities of the Indus people. The whole civilisation is often called Harappan after it.
+ganweriwal | 2600 BCE..1900 BCE | Ganweriwal | daily, power | pakistan | Ganweriwal; Indus Valley Civilisation | Ganweriwala | Ganweriwala, in the Cholistan Desert of southern Punjab, was one of the largest cities of the Indus Valley civilisation. | Ganweriwala was one of the biggest Indus cities. Today its ruins lie in a desert.
+sutkagan-dor | 2600 BCE..1900 BCE | Sutkagan Dor | trade, movement | pakistan | Sutkagan Dor; Indus Valley Civilisation | The westernmost Indus town | Sutkagan Dor, on the Makran coast near Gwadar, close to the Iranian border, is the westernmost known site of the Indus Valley civilisation. | The Indus people built towns far from their rivers. The westernmost one known is on the coast near Iran.
+pashupati-seal | 1928 | Mohenjo-daro | religion, arts, science | pakistan | Pashupati seal | The Pashupati seal is found | The Pashupati seal is a steatite seal of the Indus Valley Civilisation uncovered at Mohenjo-daro during excavations in 1928 or 1929. It shows a seated figure, possibly with three heads. | In 1928 or 1929 diggers at Mohenjo-daro found a famous little seal. It shows a seated figure that may have three heads. Nobody is sure who it is.
+shortugai | 2000 BCE | Shortugai | trade, movement | afghanistan | Shortugai | An Indus trading colony on the Oxus | Shortugai, in northern Afghanistan, was a trading colony of the Indus Valley Civilisation, established around 2000 BC on the Oxus river near the lapis lazuli mines. | The Indus people set up a trading post far away in northern Afghanistan, near the mines of a blue stone called lapis lazuli.
+ocp | 2000 BCE..1500 BCE | Sinauli | technology, daily | gangetic | Ochre Coloured Pottery culture | The Ochre Coloured Pottery culture | The Ochre Coloured Pottery culture is a Bronze Age culture of the Indo-Gangetic Plain, generally dated 2000–1500 BCE, extending from eastern Punjab to north-eastern Rajasthan and western Uttar Pradesh. | About 4,000 years ago villagers of the upper Ganges plain made a kind of pottery that archaeologists can recognise by its ochre colour.
+sinauli | 1900 BCE | Sinauli | technology, war | gangetic | Sinauli | The carts of Sinauli | Sinauli, an archaeological site in western Uttar Pradesh between the Ganga and Yamuna, gained attention for its Bronze Age vehicles with solid disk wheels, found in 2018. | In 2018 archaeologists at Sinauli dug up Bronze Age vehicles with solid disk wheels.
+cemetery-h | 1900 BCE..1300 BCE | Haryana | daily, religion | northwest | Cemetery H culture | The Cemetery H culture | The Cemetery H culture was a Bronze Age culture of the northern subcontinent, in today's Punjab, Haryana and western Uttar Pradesh, from about 1900 BCE until about 1300 BCE. | After the great Indus cities declined, a new culture took their place in the Punjab.
+pgw | 1200 BCE..600 BCE | Ghaggar-Hakra River | technology, daily | north | Painted Grey Ware culture | The Painted Grey Ware culture | The Painted Grey Ware culture is an Iron Age culture of the western Gangetic plain and the Ghaggar-Hakra valley, conventionally dated from about 1200 to 600–500 BCE. | In the early Iron Age the villages of the upper Ganges plain made fine grey pots with painted patterns.
+adichanallur | 1000 BCE..600 BCE | Adichanallur | daily, religion | south | Adichanallur | The burials of Adichanallur | Adichanallur, in the Thoothukudi district of Tamil Nadu, has been the site of very important archaeological finds. Carbon dating of samples excavated in 2004 showed they belonged to the period between 1000 BC and 600 BC. | At Adichanallur in Tamil Nadu, archaeologists found things buried between 3,000 and 2,600 years ago.
+hire-benakal | 800 BCE..200 BCE | Hire Benakal | religion, technology | deccan | Hire Benakal | The megaliths of Hire Benakal | Hire Benakal in Karnataka is a megalithic site, among the few in India that can be dated: to the period from 800 BCE to 200 BCE. | At Hire Benakal in Karnataka there are megaliths, monuments built of great stones, more than 2,000 years old.
+keeladi | 6th century BCE..3rd century BCE | Keezhadi excavation site | daily, trade, language | south | Keezhadi excavation site | The town at Keeladi | Keeladi is a settlement site of the Sangam period under excavation. A specialist on the dig dated the excavated remains to between the 6th and the 3rd century BCE. | At Keeladi in Tamil Nadu, archaeologists are digging up a town from the time of the old Tamil poems.
+arakkal | 1796 | Kannur | power, women, trade | south | Arakkal kingdom | The Arakkal Bibi signs a treaty | The Arakkal kingdom was a Muslim sultanate at Kannur in Kerala. Its king was called the Ali Raja and its ruling queen the Arakkal Beevi. After long and difficult negotiations the Bibi signed an agreement in 1796. | At Kannur in Kerala there was a small kingdom where a queen, the Arakkal Bibi, could rule. In 1796 the Bibi signed an agreement after long talks.
+port-blair | 18th century | Port Blair | power, movement | islands | Port Blair | A British base on the Andamans | In the late 18th century the British colonised the Andaman Islands and established a naval base and a penal colony on South Andaman, which was abandoned two years later. The city is now officially named Sri Vijaya Puram. | In the late 1700s the British set up a naval base and a prison settlement in the Andaman Islands. They gave it up after two years.
+battle-of-aberdeen | 1859-05-14 | Port Blair | war, power | islands | Battle of Aberdeen (Andaman Islands) | The Battle of Aberdeen | The Battle of Aberdeen, close to Port Blair, was an armed conflict on 14 May 1859 between the native people of the Andaman Islands and the forces of the British. | On 14 May 1859 the people of the Andaman Islands fought the British near Port Blair.
+cellular-jail | 1896..1906 | Port Blair | power | islands | Cellular Jail | The Cellular Jail is built | The Cellular Jail, also known as Kala Pani, is a former British colonial prison in the Andaman and Nicobar Islands, constructed between 1896 and 1906. The British had used the islands as a prison since the aftermath of the rebellion of 1857. | Between 1896 and 1906 the British built a great prison in the Andaman Islands, far across the sea. People called it Kala Pani.
+azad-hind | 1943-10 | Singapore | power, war | abroad | Azad Hind | The Provisional Government of Free India | Azad Hind, the Provisional Government of Free India, was a short-lived provisional government established in exile in Japanese-occupied Singapore in October 1943. It was controlled by Japan and has been considered a puppet government. | In October 1943 a government for a free India was set up in Singapore, which Japan had occupied.
+sentinelese | 1956 | North Sentinel Island | daily, power, nature | islands | Sentinelese | Protecting North Sentinel Island | The Sentinelese are an Indigenous people of North Sentinel Island in the Bay of Bengal. A regulation of 1956 declared the island a tribal reserve and prohibited travel within 5 kilometres of it. | The Sentinelese are the people of North Sentinel Island. Since 1956 the law has forbidden anyone to go within 5 kilometres of it.
+great-andamanese | 2010 | Andaman Islands | daily, movement, language | islands | Great Andamanese peoples | The last of the Great Andamanese | The Great Andamanese are an indigenous people of the Great Andaman archipelago who once lived throughout it, divided into many groups. Only 52 remained as of February 2010, and 59 by August 2020. | The Great Andamanese once lived all over the Andaman Islands. By 2010 there were only 52 of them left.
+barren-island | now | Barren Island (Andaman Islands) | nature | islands | Barren Island (Andaman Islands) | India's only active volcano | Barren Island, an uninhabited island in the Andaman Sea, is the only confirmed active volcano in the Indian subcontinent. | India's only active volcano is on Barren Island in the Andaman Sea. Nobody lives there.
+indira-point | now | Indira Point | nature, power | islands | Indira Point | The southernmost point of India | Indira Point, a village on Great Nicobar Island, is the southernmost point of India's territory. | The most southern place in India is Indira Point, on Great Nicobar Island.
+lakshadweep | now | Lakshadweep | nature, daily | islands | Lakshadweep | The islands of Lakshadweep | Lakshadweep, a union territory of India, is an archipelago of 36 islands with a land area of about 32.62 square kilometres. In the 2011 census 64,473 people lived on its ten inhabited islands. | Lakshadweep is a group of 36 small islands. People live on ten of them.
